TOP(1)               UNIX Programmer's Manual               TOP(1)



NAME
     top - display and update the busiest processes

SYNOPSIS
     top [ -s seconds ] [ -o column ] [ -n rows ] [ -I ] [ -h ]

DESCRIPTION
     Top shows a summary of the system and a table of its
     processes, and refreshes both until it is told to quit.  It
     must be run from a terminal; TERM names its termcap entry.

     The first line gives the host name, the time, how long the
     system has been up, the number of users logged in and the
     load averages: the number of runnable processes averaged
     over 1, 5 and 15 minutes.  Then come the processes by state,
     the share of clock ticks spent in user, nice, system and
     idle time since the previous sample, memory, and swap.

     Each process row shows:

     PID      the process ID
     USER     the owner's name
     PR       the scheduling priority
     NI       the nice value
     TEXT     the text size
     DATA     the heap's high-water break
     STACK    the bank-0 stack reserved for it
     S        R running or runnable, S sleeping, T stopped,
              Z zombie
     TIME     user plus system CPU seconds since it started
     DTIME    CPU seconds since the previous sample
     COMMAND  its name

     The first screen appears at once; the CPU percentages and
     DTIME need a second sample and say so until there is one.

OPTIONS
     -s seconds  Refresh interval, 0.1 to 1800 seconds (default
                 1).
     -o column   Sort column, 0 to 10 (default 9, DTIME then
                 TIME).
     -n rows     Show at most rows processes (0 fills the
                 screen).
     -I          Hide idle processes at first.
     -h          Print a usage summary.

KEYS
     q           Quit.
     Space       Refresh now.
     ^L          Redraw the screen.
     I or i      Show or hide idle processes.
     P           Sort by DTIME, then TIME.
     T           Sort by TIME.
     N           Sort by PID.
     M           Sort by data size.
     s           Prompt for the refresh interval (Escape
                 cancels).
     o           Prompt for the sort column (Escape cancels).
     h or ?      Show or hide the key help.

     The sort columns are 0 PID, 1 UID, 2 priority, 3 nice,
     4 text size, 5 data size, 6 stack size, 7 state, 8 TIME,
     9 DTIME and 10 command.

SEE ALSO
     ps(1), w(1), vmstat(1), getloadavg(3)

AUTHOR
     Dave Plummer, for 2.11BSD on the PDP-11.

BUGS
     The kernel keeps no nice value, text size or swap, so NI is
     always 0, TEXT is shown as -, and the swap line says none.
     Memory is the user bank pool: the banks processes hold and
     the pages still free.

     A process ID that is reused within one refresh interval may
     be charged the earlier process's DTIME, the kernel keeping
     no start time to tell the two apart.
